assistant: Subject: parse epoch-ms created_at in get_order

I could not fix the 404 from the alert in shop-mcp, so the order is still unconfirmed. The vendor's POST /orders returned 200 with order o-5002, and `create_order` passed its id, status and total on unchanged. Then the vendor's own GET /orders/o-5002 returned 404. That points to the vendor not storing the order, or not making it readable yet; nothing in our code explains it. To fix it in src/ I would have to retry, cache or invent data, which the rules forbid. It needs to go to the vendor. I did try sending the `Idempotency-Key` header that the spec recommends, but that was a guess and it broke the v1 replay test (the recording has no such header), so I reverted it. The only real change in the new 2026-10-01 version that our code mishandles is `created_at`, which now arrives as epoch milliseconds instead of ISO-8601. `get_order` parsed it with `Date.parse(String(...))`, which fails on a number and falls back to 0, so once reads work again every order would show as placed in 1970. I changed `get_order` to use numeric `created_at` values directly and keep parsing ISO strings as before, so v1 output is exactly the same. typecheck, lint and test all pass.

Subject: parse epoch-ms created_at in get_order

I could not fix the 404 from the alert in shop-mcp, so the order is still unconfirmed. The vendor's POST /orders returned 200 with order o-5002, and `create_order` passed its id, status and total on unchanged. Then the vendor's own GET /orders/o-5002 returned 404. That points to the vendor not storing the order, or not making it readable yet; nothing in our code explains it. To fix it in src/ I would have to retry, cache or invent data, which the rules forbid. It needs to go to the vendor. I did try sending the `Idempotency-Key` header that the spec recommends, but that was a guess and it broke the v1 replay test (the recording has no such header), so I reverted it. The only real change in the new 2026-10-01 version that our code mishandles is `created_at`, which now arrives as epoch milliseconds instead of ISO-8601. `get_order` parsed it with `Date.parse(String(...))`, which fails on a number and falls back to 0, so once reads work again every order would show as placed in 1970. I changed `get_order` to use numeric `created_at` values directly and keep parsing ISO strings as before, so v1 output is exactly the same. typecheck, lint and test all pass.
